; A trait method's effects, reaching the caller of its caller.
;
; `emit` is dispatched on the type of its argument, and selecting an
; implementation needs that type - which the effect fixpoint does not
; have, because it runs before any body is checked and `traitRewrite`
; rewrites the head to `Logger#Int#emit` during body checking. The
; fixpoint therefore cannot repeat the dispatch, and it does not try:
; it unions EVERY implementation instead, which is what a dynamically
; dispatched call means.
;
; Before that, a trait-method call contributed nothing. `f` still drew
; its own AX3010 - `checkAxtags` re-walks the body after the rewrite,
; so it saw the IO - but `f`'s entry stayed empty, so `g` inferred
; nothing and `symbols` printed BOTH as `#pure` with no `#effects=`.
; The diagnostic and the symbol table disagreed about the same
; function on the same run, and only `f` was ever reported: the second
; warning below is the one that was missing.
;
; The same gap turned an AX3011 into a runtime trap, which is why this
; is not only a precision fix - see 345.
(trait (Logger a) where (
  emit :: (-> a Int)
))

(impl (Logger Int) where (
  ;@axiom:effect(io)
  (emit
    (lambda (x) (__syscall3 1 1 x 0))
  )
))

;@axiom:pure
(:: f (-> Int Int))

(fn (f n) (emit n))

;@axiom:pure
(:: g (-> Int Int))

(fn (g n) (f n))

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main) (g 1))
